    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The shiny sockets in a hand tool set and the black ones sold for impact wrenches are not the same steel doing the same job in different clothes. They are engineered for opposite properties, and the failure mode when you confuse them is a socket coming apart under load rather than simply rounding off.
        </p>

        <div id="the-alloys" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the Two Alloys Actually Are
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both start as steel and both are alloyed to do a job that plain carbon steel does badly. The difference is which element gets added and what property that buys.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Chrome vanadium (Cr-V)</strong> adds chromium and vanadium. Chromium contributes hardness and corrosion resistance; vanadium refines the grain structure, which lets the steel be hardened further without becoming unpredictably brittle. The result is a hard, wear-resistant steel that holds a precise shape — which is exactly what you want on a socket that has to grip a fastener closely for twenty years of hand use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Chrome molybdenum (Cr-Mo)</strong> adds chromium and molybdenum. Molybdenum improves toughness and the steel's ability to absorb sudden shock without cracking, at the cost of some surface hardness. The result is a steel that will deform slightly under a violent load rather than fracturing.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last sentence is the whole comparison. Cr-V resists wear; Cr-Mo resists shock. Both are good steels. Neither is a cheaper version of the other, and a socket set advertising Cr-V is not making a quality claim over a Cr-Mo set — it is telling you what the sockets are for.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The One-Line Version</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Chrome vanadium for hand tools. Chrome molybdenum for impact tools.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A Cr-V socket on a hand ratchet will outlive you. The same socket on a 1/2-inch impact wrench is a projectile waiting for the right fastener — it is hard enough that it cracks instead of flexing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="hard-vs-tough" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hard Is Not the Same as Tough
          </h2>

          <p>
            In everyday speech these words are interchangeable. In metallurgy they are close to opposites, and the confusion is why people buy the wrong sockets.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hardness</strong> is resistance to deformation — how well the metal keeps its shape under pressure. A hard socket keeps its hex corners crisp, grips fasteners precisely, and resists the gradual rounding that turns an old socket into a fastener-destroying tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Toughness</strong> is resistance to fracture — how much energy the metal absorbs before it cracks. A tough socket can take a hammer blow or the rapid hammering of an impact wrench and deform slightly rather than shattering.
          </p>

          <p>
            You cannot maximise both in the same piece of steel. Pushing hardness up eventually makes a material brittle, which is why glass is very hard and not at all tough. Socket manufacturers pick a point on that trade-off according to how the tool will be driven, and the two families of socket sit on opposite sides of it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
