          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Chrome vanadium for hand tools, chrome molybdenum for impact tools.</strong> Cr-V is harder and holds its shape; Cr-Mo is tougher and survives shock. Both are good steels engineered for opposite jobs.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For home use, a good chrome set covers almost everything, and impact sockets are worth buying only in the four or five sizes your impact wrench will ever touch. <strong class="text-white">Keep the two families apart</strong> — that boundary is the entire safety story here, and the colour difference makes it easy to maintain.
          </p>
